{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"learn-bread-baking-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Learn bread baking track","description":"Teaches bread baking over a series of bakes, from a first tin loaf to shaped hearth loaves, a cold-fermented loaf and optional sourdough, reviewing each result before the next.","category":"baking","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["learn","plan","review"],"role":["home-cook"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["preferences","text","image"],"output":["plan","questions","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"mid","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"basic","tags":["bread-making","learning-path","yeasted-dough","dough-shaping","cold-fermentation"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["plan-bread-bake-schedule","convert-to-bakers-percentages","start-sourdough-starter","read-sourdough-crumb"],"personas":["baking-instructor"]},"args":[{"name":"baker","description":"Your experience, what bread you want to be able to make, how often you can bake, room temperature, equipment (scale, mixer, lidded pot, tins) and flour you can buy.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"last_bake","description":"If you are returning, the step you reached and how your last loaf turned out. Optional.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"first-loaf","file":"steps/01-first-loaf.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"review-first","file":"steps/02-review-first.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"hearth-loaf","file":"steps/03-hearth-loaf.md","stage":"learn","gate":"approve"},{"id":"cold-ferment","file":"steps/04-cold-ferment.md","stage":"learn","gate":"approve"},{"id":"next-path","file":"steps/05-next-path.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Teaches bread the way a good class does: one bake at a time, each building one new skill on the last, with the baker's real result reviewed before moving on. Each step plans or reviews one bake and stops; the next step starts only when the baker reports back.\n\n<baker>\n{{baker}}\n</baker>\n{{#last_bake}}\nReturning baker:\n<last_bake>\n{{last_bake}}\n</last_bake>\nResume at the step it points to and start with a short review.\n{{/last_bake}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- Weights in grams with baker's percentages; times as ranges tied to dough temperature and cues (rise, feel, poke test, internal temperature about 93 to 96 °C for lean loaves).\n- Introduce one new skill per bake and change one variable at a time.\n- If the baker sends a photo or description, describe what you see before judging it.\n- Safety: oven gloves for lidded pots and steam trays, never pour water onto glass oven doors or a hot glass dish.\n- If the baker wants to skip ahead, confirm once, then adapt.","steps":[{"id":"first-loaf","file":"steps/01-first-loaf.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: First loaf\n\nPlan a simple yeasted tin loaf that teaches mixing, kneading or folding, judging a rise and baking to temperature.\n\n1. If the baker text lacks a scale, room temperature or a tin, ask in one short message and stop.\n2. Give a formula of about 500 g flour at around 65% hydration, 2% salt and instant yeast adjusted to their room temperature.\n3. Steps with cues: mix, knead or rest-and-fold (with a windowpane check), bulk rise until about doubled, shape a tin loaf with surface tension, proof until the poke test springs back slowly, bake and check internal temperature, cool fully before slicing.\n4. A short \"What to notice\" list for the baker to report: how long each rise took, dough feel, oven spring, crumb, crust.\n\nWrite it as Formula (table), Method, Timeline, What to notice. Stop and ask them to bake and report back."},{"id":"review-first","file":"steps/02-review-first.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Review the first loaf\n\n1. Ask for their report and a photo of the cut loaf if they have not given one, then stop.\n2. Describe what you see, then diagnose the main issue (under- or over-proofing, weak shaping, underbaking, cutting too soon) tied to their notes and room temperature.\n3. Name what went well and one change to carry forward.\n4. Decide readiness: if the loaf had a major fault, suggest repeating step 1 with the one change; otherwise move to step 3.\n\nWrite it as What I see, Diagnosis, Carry forward, Next. Stop for approval."},{"id":"hearth-loaf","file":"steps/03-hearth-loaf.md","stage":"learn","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Shaped hearth loaf\n\nNew skills: a wetter dough (about 70 to 72%), stretch-and-folds instead of kneading, pre-shaping and shaping a boule or batard, a banneton or bowl with a floured towel, scoring, and steam (a lidded heavy pot, or a tray of hot water with an oven stone or steel).\n\n1. Formula and schedule fitted to their day and temperature.\n2. Method with cues for folds, bulk rise (about 50 to 75% rise in a straight-sided container), pre-shape, bench rest, shaping, proofing, scoring angle and depth, baking lid on then lid off.\n3. A \"What to notice\" list, then stop for their report. When they report, review it as in step 2 before going on."},{"id":"cold-ferment","file":"steps/04-cold-ferment.md","stage":"learn","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Cold-fermented loaf\n\nNew skills: less yeast and a long cold proof (or a poolish made the night before) for flavour and a flexible schedule.\n\n1. Explain in two lines what time and cold do to flavour and crust.\n2. Formula with the reduced yeast or poolish, and a schedule that splits the work over two days around their routine.\n3. Method with cues, including baking straight from the fridge and judging a cold proof.\n4. A \"What to notice\" list, then stop for their report and review it as in step 2."},{"id":"next-path","file":"steps/05-next-path.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Next path\n\n1. Summarise their progress in three or four lines: skills learned, their usual timings at their temperature, their repeatable base formula.\n2. Offer two or three paths matched to their goal: sourdough (start a starter, then a first sourdough loaf), enriched breads (brioche, challah, rolls), whole-grain and rye breads, or flatbreads and pizza.\n3. For the chosen path, outline the next three bakes with the one new skill in each.\n4. A one-page cheat sheet: base formula, cue checklist and troubleshooting table (Problem | Cause | Fix)."}]}
